 a dopamine D2 receptor antagonist**. In this case, **Risperidone** (frequently used for diabetic gastroparesis or as an antiemetic) is the culprit. Other common offending agents include antipsychotics (haloperidol, risperidone), prochlorperazine, and fluphenazine. The first-line and definitive management is to **discontinue the offending medication**. Parkinsonian symptoms typically resolve within weeks to months after withdrawal. If prokinetic therapy is still required for gastroparesis, safe alternatives like erythromycin or domperidone (which does not cross the blood-brain barrier) should be considered.'),
