    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-old female is rushed to the emergency department after experiencing a sudden, dramatic onset of severe gait instability and 2 falls 5 hours ago. The patient reports severe room-spinning vertigo, nausea, and a persistent headache localized to the back of her head. History is notable for long-standing hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Physical examination reveals:<br>• Cardiovascular: <b>BP 177/95 mmHg, HR 82 bpm</b><br>• Gait/Coordination: <b>Inability to stand or walk without support; prominent dysmetria (past-pointing) on bilateral finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing</b><br>• Neurological: <b>Alert and oriented; motor strength is 5/5 in all extremities; cranial nerves and sensory exams are completely intact</b><br>• Romberg test: <b>Unable to perform (unsteady even with eyes open)</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the highest priority next step in management?', '["Acute cerebellar stroke; perform an urgent non-contrast CT scan of the brain (or brain MRI) and consult stroke neurology for emergency reperfusion or decompressive surgery evaluation", "Meniere''s disease; administer a 40 mg intravenous bolus of furosemide, restrict dietary sodium, and refer to ENT", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus; perform an emergent high-volume lumbar puncture (CSF tap test) at the bedside to lower intracranial pressure", "Acute vestibular neuritis; initiate a 10-day course of high-dose oral prednisone and advise bed rest for 48 hours", "Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV); perform the Epley maneuver immediately in the emergency department and discharge the patient with oral meclizine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient presents with **acute-onset gait ataxia, vertigo, and headache**, which is highly concerning for an **acute cerebellar stroke** (ischemic or hemorrhagic in the posterior circulation). In an older patient with cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes), the sudden onset of severe gait instability (inability to stand or walk without support) and **ipsilateral cerebellar signs** (limb dysmetria on finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing) is a medical emergency. Unlike peripheral vestibular disorders (like vestibular neuritis or BPPV) where patients can typically stand and walk with assistance, patients with central cerebellar lesions have severe truncal instability and are often **unable to stand even with their eyes open**. The immediate priority is an **urgent non-contrast CT scan of the brain** (to rule out hemorrhage or mass effect/herniation risk) or a **brain MRI** (diffusion-weighted imaging is gold-standard for early ischemic stroke), followed by consultation with **stroke neurology**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-old male is evaluated in the clinic after experiencing 4 episodes of sudden loss of consciousness (syncope) over the past 3 months. The patient''s spouse notes that during the most recent episode, the patient was backing the car out of the driveway, turned his head sharply to look behind, and immediately slumped over and fell forward onto the steering wheel. The patient recovered fully within 1 minute with no confusion. The patient also reports a similar episode while shaving and wearing a tight dress shirt. Ophthalmic, cardiac, and basic orthostatic examinations are completely normal. The physician performs a supervised **carotid sinus massage** in the clinic with continuous EKG and blood pressure monitoring, which demonstrates:<br>• EKG: <b>A transient period of asystole (ventricular standstill) lasting 3.4 seconds</b><br>• Blood pressure: <b>A temporary drop in systolic blood pressure of 54 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the first-line treatment of choice for this symptomatic condition?', '["Carotid sinus hypersensitivity (cardioinhibitory subtype); dual-chamber permanent cardiac pacemaker placement", "Sick sinus syndrome; immediate percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) and coronary artery bypass grafting", "Orthostatic hypotension; taper and discontinue all blood pressure medications and wear full-length compression stockings", "Vasovagal syncope; daily oral low-dose fludrocortisone and avoidance of all head rotation", "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity (vasodepressor subtype); lifelong high-dose oral midodrine and strict sodium loading"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has **Carotid Sinus Hypersensitivity (CSH)**, an underdiagnosed cause of syncope and falls in older adults. It is characterized by an exaggerated response to stimulation of the carotid sinus baroreceptors (which can be triggered by neck turning, looking up, shaving, or wearing tight shirt collars). CSH is diagnosed via supervised **carotid sinus massage** showing: (1) a **cardioinhibitory response** (asystole/ventricular standstill lasting ≥ 3 seconds), and/or (2) a **vasodepressor response** (a drop in systolic blood pressure of ≥ 50 mmHg in the absence of significant bradycardia). The patient''s massage demonstrates a **cardioinhibitory response** with a 3.4-second asystole and a drop in blood pressure. For patients with symptomatic, recurrent syncope due to the cardioinhibitory subtype of CSH, the first-line, gold-standard treatment is the **surgical implantation of a dual-chamber permanent cardiac pacemaker**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-old female is brought to the clinic by her daughter due to a recent nocturnal fall. The daughter reports finding the patient on the floor of the hallway at 2:00 AM, looking confused and unsteady on her feet, while trying to walk to the bathroom. The patient has a history of mild osteoarthritis and has suffered from chronic sleep onset insomnia for years, for which she has been taking **Metoclopramide** nightly for the past 3 years. Physical and neurological examinations are normal. According to the **AGS Beers Criteria** for Potentially Inappropriate Medication Use in Older Adults, what is the best explanation for this patient''s clinical presentation, and what is the most appropriate management?', '["Vestibular hypofunction; the drug is completely safe; prescribe daily oral meclizine and recommend a soft walker for nocturnal mobilization", "Normal age-related nocturia; the falls are caused exclusively by rapid movement in the dark; prescribe nightly oral desmopressin and continue the nightly Metoclopramide", "Beers criteria medication side effect (Metoclopramide); immediately discontinue the medication abruptly without a taper, and initiate high-dose daily oral diphenhydramine", "Offending medication toxicity (Metoclopramide); older adults have decreased clearance of benzodiazepines/Z-drugs, leading to daytime drowsiness, psychomotor impairment, and falls; perform a gradual medication taper and discontinue, and initiate Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I)", "Idiopathic insomnia syndrome; the patient''s falls are a direct complication of inadequate sleep; increase the nightly dose of Metoclopramide by 50% to ensure deeper sleep"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient''s nocturnal fall and unsteady gait are direct side effects of **Metoclopramide** (a benzodiazepine or non-benzodiazepine receptor agonist/Z-drug). The **AGS Beers Criteria** strongly advises against the use of **benzodiazepines** (e.g., alprazolam, temazepam, lorazepam) and **non-benzodiazepine receptor agonists/Z-drugs** (e.g., zolpidem, eszopiclone, zaleplon) in older adults. With aging, physiological pharmacokinetic shifts (increased volume of distribution for lipophilic drugs, decreased hepatic clearance) result in prolonged drug half-lives. This leads to **cognitive clouding**, **daytime somnolence**, **impaired psychomotor performance**, ataxia, and **a two-fold increased risk of falls, fractures, and motor vehicle accidents**. The best management is to **gradually taper and discontinue** the drug to avoid withdrawal seizures or severe rebound insomnia. First-line therapy for chronic insomnia in older adults is non-pharmacological: **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I)**. Clinicians should never prescribe diphenhydramine as an alternative, as it is a highly anticholinergic Beers Criteria drug that also dramatically increases falls risk.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-old man is brought to the outpatient clinic by his family due to a 9-month history of multiple unexplained falls, most recently occurring when he tried to turn around quickly. The patient''s family notes that he has become increasingly slow-moving and quiet. History is notable for type 2 diabetes mellitus complicated by gastroparesis, for which he was prescribed Metoclopramide 8 months ago. The patient is on a total of 11 daily medications. Physical examination reveals:<br>• Gait: <b>Shuffling, short-stepped gait with decreased arm swing, and significant postural instability (falls backward on the pull test)</b><br>• Motor exam: <b>Mild resting tremor in the right hand and bilateral cogwheel rigidity</b><br>• Mental status: <b>Alert and oriented to person, place, and time; no cognitive decline</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Vascular parkinsonism; initiate aggressive antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel, and check a lipid panel", "Idiopathic Parkinson''s disease; initiate high-dose levodopa-carbidopa therapy immediately and schedule a brain MRI", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus; perform a diagnostic high-volume lumbar puncture (CSF tap test) to evaluate for shunt placement", "Drug-induced parkinsonism secondary to Metoclopramide; taper and discontinue the offending medication, and transition to a safe prokinetic alternative if gastroparesis symptoms recur", "Progressive supranuclear palsy; initiate baclofen therapy for spasticity and refer the patient for speech therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient presents with classic features of **drug-induced parkinsonism (DIP)**. DIP is the second most common cause of parkinsonism in the elderly and is frequently misdiagnosed as idiopathic Parkinson''s disease. The key clues are the **subacute onset** of symmetric parkinsonian features (shuffling gait, bradykinesia, cogwheel rigidity, postural instability) and the **use of a dopamine D2 receptor antagonist**. In this case, **Metoclopramide** (frequently used for diabetic gastroparesis or as an antiemetic) is the culprit. Other common offending agents include antipsychotics (haloperidol, risperidone), prochlorperazine, and fluphenazine. The first-line and definitive management is to **discontinue the offending medication**. Parkinsonian symptoms typically resolve within weeks to months after withdrawal. If prokinetic therapy is still required for gastroparesis, safe alternatives like erythromycin or domperidone (which does not cross the blood-brain barrier) should be considered.');
